aphrase holdout for the ' Expl' fragment of Explained in a scientific context unlike the financial examples.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"THE CLERK ENTERED THE FILINGS INTO THE DOCKET AND CLOSED THE FOLDER.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":1.363029,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Upper-case ' FIL' fragment of FILINGS in a new sentence and a new court context, which the hypothesis predicts fires.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"THE STATED PURPOSE OF THE TRIAL WAS TO MEASURE HOW QUICKLY THE COATING WEARS.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":1.171266,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Upper-case ' PUR' fragment of PURPOSE in a new scientific context about a coating trial.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"THIS PARTICULAR SPECIMEN COMES FROM A CELLULAR LAYER NEAR THE SURFACE.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":1.006748,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"The 'ULAR' fragment in upper-case PARTICULAR and CELLULAR in a new laboratory context, claimed as one of the strong triggers.","measured":true},{"kind":"positive_holdout","prompt":"The tide came in over the flats a little after four, and the crew woke to the sound of water moving under the boards of the shed. Mara pulled on her boots, checked the rope on the skiff, and walked the length of the jetty with a lamp held low so as not to wake the others. Nobody had slept well. The wind had turned during the night and the old buoy had come loose somewhere past the point, so the first hour went to dragging the channel for it with a hook and a length of chain. By the time they found it the sun was up and the gulls had started in on the gutting tables behind the shed. They ate bread and cold fish standing up, and then the work of the day began in earnest: driving the stakes along the eastern edge of the flat, measuring the drop left by the last storm, and writing every reading into the ruled book that lived in a tin box under the seat. Ord, who had done the same work for thirty years, said the flat had moved further this winter than any he could remember, and that the book would show it if anybody cared to look. In the afternoon a party came down from the county in two cars, and there was a long argument about the boundary line that nobody settled. The visitors left before the light went. Mara stayed out on the flat until the water reached her knees, then came in, dried her hands on her trousers, and opened the book again. The last page had three columns and a note in her own hand from the spring. She sat with it under the lamp and worked through the numbers twice, and then a third time, because the third column would not agree. The physics of the tide was simpler than the politics of the boundary, she thought, and the mathematics had never once lied to her. Photography had helped in the early years, when the plates were cheap and the light was good; now the soundings were taken with technology she did not fully trust, and the information went up to a machine in the county office where nobody read it. She copied the figures out anyway, sealed the book in its tin, and went to bed. Before the lamp was out she heard the skiff knock against the jetty once and then settle. In the morning the county men would come back with their own map and their own ideas about where the line fell, and Ord would say what he always said, that the water had been there before any of them and would be there after. She did not expect the argument to end. The flat would still be where it was, and the book would still hold what it held, whichever way the men decided to draw their line. She slept badly and woke before the light, and the whole business of measuring began again from the beginning.","passed":true,"expected_active":true,"activation":1.282706,"expected_delta":null,"observed_delta":null,"detail":"Long-context holdout of about 650 to